  "worked_solution": "The total derivative of a vector-valued function $f:R\\to {R}^{m}$ with respect to a scalar $x$ is the column vector\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{m}}{∂x}\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{m\\times 1}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the function \n\n- acts on a scalar $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- outputs a vector $f(x)\\in {R}^{2}$ defined by \n\n$$\nf(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}]\\in {R}^{2\\times 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the total derivative at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
